# --- SSH signing trust list ---
#
# `create_` prefix: chezmoi writes this file once and then NEVER overwrites
# it, so your real entries survive every future `chezmoi apply`. Edit it in
# place on each machine.
#
# Safe to commit if you want to: it holds only public keys and an address
# you already publish in every commit header. It is designed to be shared.
#
# WHY IT MUST EXIST AT ALL. Without gpg.ssh.allowedSignersFile pointing at
# a real file, `git log --show-signature` on a correctly signed commit
# reports "No signature" rather than an error. Local verification silently
# degrades to nothing and you cannot tell a signed commit from an unsigned
# one.
#
# FORMAT
#   <principal> [options] <keytype> <base64-key>
#
# ADD YOUR SIGNING KEY:
#   ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C "signing" -f ~/.ssh/id_signing_ed25519
#   printf '%s %s\n' "24684994+tannergolden@users.noreply.github.com" \
#     "$(cat ~/.ssh/id_signing_ed25519.pub | cut -d' ' -f1,2)" \
#     >> ~/.config/git/allowed_signers
#
# Then register the PUBLIC half on GitHub under Settings, SSH and GPG keys,
# as a SIGNING key. That is a separate registration from an authentication
# key; a key added only for authentication will sign commits that GitHub
# then shows as Unverified.
#
# PIN THE NAMESPACE. `namespaces="git"` stops a signature made over a file
# being replayed as a commit signature. Verified: signing with -n git and
# verifying with -n file is rejected.
#
#   me@example.com namespaces="git" ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3Nz...
#
# KEY ROTATION, WITHOUT LOSING OLD COMMITS. Git evaluates the validity
# window against the COMMIT time, not the current time, so an old key can
# be retired while every commit it signed stays verifiable forever:
#
#   me@example.com namespaces="git",valid-before="20270101" ssh-ed25519 AAAA...old
#   me@example.com namespaces="git",valid-after="20270101"  ssh-ed25519 AAAA...new
#
# That is offline, in-repository, revocable key rotation with no secret
# manager involved.
#
# ONE THING THIS FILE DOES NOT DO. It answers "was this signed by a key I
# trust", NOT "was this signed by the person in the Author field". Git
# reports the principal from THIS file and still returns a good signature
# even when that principal differs from the commit author. Binding a key to
# an identity is the forge's job.
